---
import SiteLayout from '../layouts/SiteLayout.astro';
import { repository, siteUrl } from '../../lib/paths.mjs';
---
<SiteLayout title="ElevenClock-rs: your own clock format on the Windows 11 taskbar" landing>
  <div class="hero-body" slot="hero">
    <h1>Your clock.<br><span>Your format.</span></h1>
    <p class="lead">ElevenClock-rs puts your own date and time format on the Windows 11
      taskbar: ISO week dates, seconds, a second line, whatever you need.
      Native, tiny and free.</p>
    <div class="cta">
      <a class="button primary" href={`${repository}/releases/latest`}>Download for Windows 11</a>
      <a class="button" href={repository}>View the source</a>
    </div>
    <p class="fineprint">Free and open source · Windows 11 x64 · No administrator rights</p>
  </div>

  <figure class="desktop" slot="hero" aria-label="A Windows 11 taskbar showing the ElevenClock-rs clock">
    <div class="taskbar">
      <div class="apps" aria-hidden="true">
        <span class="app start"></span><span class="app"></span><span class="app"></span>
        <span class="app"></span><span class="app"></span>
      </div>
      <div class="tray">
        <span class="tray-icons" aria-hidden="true">⌃ &nbsp;◔ &nbsp;🔈</span>
        <span class="clock" id="hero-clock">2642.2</span>
      </div>
    </div>
    <figcaption id="hero-caption">ISO week date: 2026, week 42, Tuesday</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <main id="main" tabindex="-1">
    <section id="features" class="features">
      <h2>Everything a taskbar clock should be</h2>
      <div class="grid">
        <article>
          <h3>Any format you like</h3>
          <p>Pick a preset or write your own with simple tokens: hours, minutes, seconds,
            weekdays, months, calendar years and ISO weeks, on one line or two.
            A live preview checks it before it reaches your taskbar.</p>
        </article>
        <article>
          <h3>Featherlight</h3>
          <p>About <strong>3 MiB</strong> of memory and around <strong>0.01% CPU</strong>.
            No busy loop: the clock only redraws when its text changes.</p>
        </article>
        <article>
          <h3>Never breaks your clock</h3>
          <p>The Windows clock is never changed or disabled. ElevenClock-rs draws on top
            of it, so clicking still opens the calendar, and exiting brings the
            original straight back.</p>
        </article>
        <article>
          <h3>Every display</h3>
          <p>Show your clock on the primary taskbar or on every monitor, at any scaling.
            It follows auto-hide, full-screen apps and Explorer restarts.</p>
        </article>
        <article>
          <h3>One small native app</h3>
          <p>Written in Rust as a single executable: no Python, Qt or runtime to install.
            Installs for your account without administrator rights, or runs portable.</p>
        </article>
        <article>
          <h3>Private and accessible</h3>
          <p>No internet access and no telemetry. Works with screen readers, the
            keyboard and high contrast. Light and dark themes follow Windows.</p>
        </article>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section id="try" class="try">
      <h2>Try a format</h2>
      <p>This is the same format language the app uses. Pick a preset or type your own.</p>
      <div class="presets" role="group" aria-label="Presets">
        <button type="button" data-format="%g%V.%u">ISO week date</button>
        <button type="button" data-format="%y%V.%u">Calendar year + ISO week</button>
        <button type="button" data-format="%H:%M&#10;%g%V.%u">Time + ISO week date</button>
        <button type="button" data-format="%a %d %b %H:%M:%S">Weekday and seconds</button>
      </div>
      <div class="playground">
        <label for="format">Format <span class="hint">(Enter for a second line)</span></label>
        <textarea id="format" rows="2" spellcheck="false">%H:%M&#10;%g%V.%u</textarea>
        <div class="preview">
          <div class="taskbar small">
            <div class="tray"><span class="clock" id="preview-clock" aria-live="off"></span></div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <details class="tokens">
        <summary>All tokens</summary>
        <table>
          <tr><td><code>%H</code> <code>%I</code></td><td>Hour, 24 / 12 hour</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>%M</code> <code>%S</code> <code>%p</code></td><td>Minute, second, AM/PM</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>%Y</code> <code>%y</code></td><td>Calendar year, full / two digits</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>%G</code> <code>%g</code></td><td>ISO week-year, full / two digits</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>%V</code> <code>%u</code></td><td>ISO week 01–53; weekday Monday=1 to Sunday=7</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>%m</code> <code>%d</code></td><td>Month, day</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>%a</code> <code>%A</code></td><td>Weekday, short / full</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>%b</code> <code>%B</code></td><td>Month name, short / full</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>%%</code></td><td>A literal percent sign</td></tr>
        </table>
      </details>
    </section>

    <section class="shot">
      <div>
        <h2>Settings that explain themselves</h2>
        <p>Every change is previewed against tricky dates, like the week that spans New
          Year. A format that is invalid or does not fit the taskbar is explained and
          never applied. Apply, Cancel and Reset work the way you expect.</p>
        <p>Start at sign-in is off until you turn it on, and diagnostics are only ever
          copied to your clipboard, never sent anywhere.</p>
      </div>
      <img src={siteUrl('docs/design/images/settings/first-run.png')} width="611" height="624" loading="lazy"
           alt="The ElevenClock-rs Settings window with the Time + ISO week date preset, a live preview and the token list">
    </section>

    <section id="install" class="install">
      <h2>Install in a minute</h2>
      <ol>
        <li>Download <code>ElevenClock-rs-…-x64-setup.exe</code> from the
          <a href={`${repository}/releases/latest`}>latest release</a>.</li>
        <li>Run it. It installs for your account only, without administrator rights.</li>
        <li>Open <strong>ElevenClock-rs</strong> from the Start menu and pick your format.</li>
      </ol>
      <p>Prefer no installer? The release also has a <strong>portable ZIP</strong>: extract
        it and double-click <code>elevenclock-rs.exe</code>.</p>
      <p class="note">Releases are not code-signed yet, so Windows may say <em>“Windows
        protected your PC”</em>. Choose <strong>More info</strong>, then
        <strong>Run anyway</strong>. Each release lists SHA-256 checksums.</p>
      <p>Coming from the original ElevenClock? Quit it and switch it off in
        <strong>Settings &gt; Apps &gt; Startup</strong> first; the
        <a href={siteUrl('docs/migration/')}>migration guide</a>
        explains what changed.</p>
    </section>

    <section id="faq" class="faq">
      <h2>Questions</h2>
      <details>
        <summary>Does it change or hide the Windows clock?</summary>
        <p>No. It draws its own clock on top of the Windows one and never modifies,
          disables or hides it. Exit ElevenClock-rs, or pause it from the tray, and
          the Windows clock is right there.</p>
      </details>
      <details>
        <summary>What do I need?</summary>
        <p>Stock Windows 11 on an x64 PC. Windows 10, ARM64 devices, Insider builds,
          taskbar replacements such as ExplorerPatcher or StartAllBack, and taskbars on
          the side or top of the screen are not supported.</p>
      </details>
      <details>
        <summary>What is an ISO week date?</summary>
        <p>A compact way to write a day as year, week and weekday: <code>2642.2</code>
          is 2026, week 42, Tuesday. Weeks start on Monday and week 1 contains the
          year's first Thursday, so the week-year can differ from the calendar year
          around New Year.</p>
      </details>
      <details>
        <summary>How does it relate to the original ElevenClock?</summary>
        <p>ElevenClock-rs is an independent Rust rewrite, inspired by
          <a href="https://github.com/marticliment/ElevenClock">ElevenClock</a> by Martí
          Climent. It focuses on a custom format with a tiny footprint; some features of
          the original, like internet time sync and translations, are not included. It
          is not affiliated with or endorsed by the original project.</p>
      </details>
      <details>
        <summary>How do I uninstall it?</summary>
        <p>Choose <strong>Exit ElevenClock-rs</strong> in Settings or the tray menu, then
          uninstall from <strong>Windows Settings &gt; Apps &gt; Installed apps</strong>.
          It also removes its own startup entry.</p>
      </details>
      <details>
        <summary>Something is wrong. Where do I get help?</summary>
        <p>Open <strong>Settings &gt; Support</strong>, click <strong>Copy diagnostics</strong>
          and paste it into a <a href={`${repository}/issues/new`}>new issue</a>.
          The report contains no user name, paths or window titles.</p>
      </details>
    </section>
  </main>

  <script>
    import '../scripts/clock.js';
  </script>
</SiteLayout>
